s  is called State and transition explosion  and that's when you have more and more  States and more and more Transitions and  since a lot of these can connect to  others you have a combinatorial  explosion of these states and  Transitions and it could be a little  unwieldy and start to look like this  spaghetti soup over here and hard to  visualize and so that's why there's a  formalism called State charts which  extends the notion of State machines and  provides a lot of other features so one  of the features it provides is this  notion of actions or these effects that  you could attach to Transitions and so  these are transition actions but you  could also simplify these by putting  them inside of the states and saying  whenever I enter the state we're going  to fetch data whenever I exit we're  going to log Telemetry and so all of  these action are part of the formalism  of State charts  and so some other important things  there's compound states where you could  Nest States inside of a parent state so  when I transitioned to the ready States  we're in the playing State  um and then when when I transition out  then you know we're in the loading  States  um and so you could transition between  these playing and pause dates once  you're in the parent States there's also  parallel states which allow you to  specify different regions or  responsibilities within your app so if I  have a music player I might have two  responsibilities which is the player  itself whether it's playing or paused  and the volume whether it's muted or  unmuted and so there's a ton of other  features like guarded transitions the  ability to raise events or send events  to self delayed transitions event list  transitions where a transition is  immediately taken history States and a  lot more and so um I have a quick one  more demo for you and so with this we're  going to be going the other way so  instead of of directly creating the  state machine over here we are or sorry  instead of working with the same machine  over here we're going to be creating a  state machine and this is going to be a  much simpler app but you're going to see  still how State machines are useful in  this app what I want to do is when I  click this video I want it to open up  full screen and start playing and then  when I exit the video and there's a  number of ways I could exit it I want it  to stop playing but also once the video  stops I want it to stop playing or and  go back to the mini mode after a few  seconds  so let's go ahead and model this right  now so I um right now this is actually  stately.ai editor which is in a uh a  free editor for State machines and so I  have this mini State over here I'm going  to create a full State over here and so  this is when the video player is full so  um I'm just going to say full screen  video player and just have that over  here  and so this is going to be toggled when  let's just make a toggle event  so I could toggle back and forth between  mini and full salt just put toggle over  here  but there's a couple of other events  that could happen that causes me to go  back to the mini state so for example  I'll just move this up here uh when the  video  ends  then we need to go back to the mini  State and also  when the Escape key is pressed so I have  this essay in Escape key event over here  now in order to get these events we also  have to invoke some listeners basically  or actors in other words so what I'm  going to do is I'm going to just add  these so we're going to be listening for  the video ended event and we're also  going to be listening for the key Escape  events and so these are going to be  things that just while we're in this  dates it's going to be listening for the  video ending events and the key Escape  events  and at the same time we also want to  play and pause the video too so when we  enter this full screen State we want to  play the video  and when we exit we want to pause the  video  um okay so I think that looks pretty  good except we were talking about State  charts so what I'm going to do is also  add a couple of child States so this is  going to be playing  and  we also want to have some sort of delay  when the video is stopped  so now instead of this video ended event  being here we actually want that video  ended events to be right over here so  that we could wait a couple seconds  before we go back to the mini state so  I'm going to just make this an after  transition so we're going to say after  and let's set it to two seconds so 2 000  milliseconds  all right now let's actually make sure  that this logic works I'm going to click  simulate over here and so we could you  know play pretend with our live diagram  that we made and let's toggle back and  forth once we toggle we're going back  between the mini and the full screen and  also when we're in the full screen we  could of course press the Escape key  which should take us back to the Mini  screen  but if we're watching the video and the  video ends then after two seconds we  should transition back to the Mini  screen okay so now that we have our  diagram in place let's go ahead and  start coding it and to do that all I'm  going to do is export the code because I  don't have that much time so we're just  going to copy all of this to the  clipboard and then I'm going to put this  in our machine over here and hopefully  it works all right so now I have my  logic and I've already attached event  listeners and things like that so if I  click here  not working  let's see oh I it literally says David  don't forget this so uh oops  there we go so now we're adding an event  listener to the uh to the click and so  now when I click  and I toggle out it's going to close the  video  also when I press the Escape key  it closes it and when I allow the video  to end hopefully after two seconds one  two  it goes back to many so there you go  and so that's just one example of how  visual programming can actually be  really helpful because we're not only  making it easier to um to express  application logic and to create it but  we're making it easier for other people  on the team to understand it and by  other people I don't just mean  non-developers I mean the rest of the  developers on your team because the way  that we typically code is we put logic  and event handlers and callbacks and  just some ad hoc logic floating around  everywhere and so with the rise of Redux  and flux and these types of events  driven Frameworks we've begun to be a  little bit smarter and put all of these  in reducers instead where events might  change some data within the reducer  Steam Machines take that a little bit  further and instead they separate this  data into different states and they give  you a more structured way of organizing  the different states and transitions  that comprise your application logic and  of course State charts take that even  further allowing you to describe  declarative effects to Nest states to  make them parallel and to handle a lot  more than normal State machines and then  we could get even further so uh we could  like one state chart describes the  behavior of a single entity but we could  have a lot of entities talking to each  other and these are called actors now  actors are things that could send and  receive events and we don't have too  much time to go into this right now but  an actor model if you've been working in  the front end you might not be familiar  but in the back end with microservices  and things like that the actor model is  actually really prevalent there's a lot  of rules that actors may have where it's  basically entities sending and receiving  events to each other and so one of the  best ways I have of describing like what  an actor is is like ordering a coffee  you have two different actors the a  barista in the person who's ordering a  coffee and in order for that transaction  to take place they actually have to talk  to each other so this idea of message  passing extends Beyond just normal human  communication it's actually a really  good way of expressing how different  parts of your code your applications  your microservices every part of your  code base can talk to each other and so  that's why I think having things in an  events driven way is really good because  by using events you have these arrows  which can visually describe just how  everything interacts with each other and  you know besides State machines and  state charts you also have you know  actor system architectures and sequence  diagrams and these allow you to really  take the requirements that you're given  by designers and project managers and  code in a simple visual way and make it  really easy to express your application  lot  so in general whether you're coding  um you know just front end back-end  applications no matter how much  complexity there is using these visual  diagrams can really help you simplify  the application logic and make it so  that your entire team can understand it  and that's why I think the future of  development is visual so with that thank  you very much orda
 

